<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alan Turing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alan Turing was an English mathematician and computer scientist. His 1936 paper on computable numbers laid the theoretical foundations of computing, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he worked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bletchley-park">Bletchley Park</a>, where he led work on breaking German naval Enigma ciphers. Prosecuted for homosexuality in 1952, he died in 1954 and received a posthumous royal pardon in 2013.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alan-turing.jpg" alt="Alan Turing" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elliott &amp; Fry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alan_turing_header.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alan Mathison Turing was born in Maida Vale, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, on 23 June 1912, while his father, Julius Mathison Turing, was on leave from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a>. His mother was Ethel Sara Turing, née Stoney, of an Anglo-Irish family. At Sherborne School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, which he entered in 1926, he formed a close friendship with Christopher Morcom, who has been described as his first love; Morcom died of complications of bovine tuberculosis in February 1930, shortly before graduation.</p><p>Turing sat the King's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> scholarship examination in mathematics in 1930 and graduated from King's. He earned a doctorate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeton-university">Princeton University</a> in 1938. On returning to Cambridge he attended the 1939 lectures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-wittgenstein">Ludwig Wittgenstein</a> on the foundations of mathematics, where the two disagreed, Turing defending formalism and Wittgenstein holding that mathematics invents its truths rather than discovering absolute ones.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From September 1938 Turing worked part-time with the Government Code and Cypher School and, with Dilly Knox, studied the Enigma cipher machine of Nazi Germany. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bletchley-park">Bletchley Park</a> he specified an electromechanical machine, the bombe, which could break Enigma more effectively than the Polish bomba kryptologiczna from which its name derived; with an enhancement by Gordon Welchman it became a main tool against Enigma messages. He took on the German naval Enigma as head of Hut 8, solving the essential part of its indicator system in December 1939 and devising Banburismus, a sequential statistical technique. In July 1942 he devised Turingery against the Lorenz cipher.</p><p>After the war he joined the National Physical Laboratory, and on 19 February 1946 presented the first detailed design of a stored-program computer, the Automatic Computing Engine. In 1948 he joined Max Newman's Computing Machine Laboratory at the University of Manchester. In 1951 he turned to mathematical biology and published The Chemical Basis of Morphogenesis in January 1952, proposing that chemicals reacting and diffusing together, a reaction-diffusion system, could account for the main phenomena of morphogenesis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1952, after acknowledging a sexual relationship with another man, Turing was prosecuted for gross indecency. He received probation conditional on chemical castration and lost his security clearance. He died on 7 June 1954, aged 41, of cyanide poisoning, and the inquest ruled the death a suicide; his family and biographers have variously accepted, questioned or disputed that verdict.</p><p>His wartime work, covered by the Official Secrets Act, went unrecognised in his lifetime. Following a 2009 campaign, Prime Minister Gordon Brown apologised for the appalling way he was treated, and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> granted him a pardon in 2013. The Alan Turing law, part of a 2017 law, retroactively pardoned men cautioned or convicted under the criminalisation of homosexual acts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alan Turing” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its resistance to Nazi Germany in 1940–1941, when it was the only major power still fighting, was decisive for the outcome of the Second World War, and the codebreakers of Bletchley Park, among them <b>Alan Turing</b>, contributed to Allied victory and to the birth of computing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Maida Vale, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Wilmslow, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.53,-0.19&amp;year=1939" aria-label="Maida Vale on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alan Turing"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4990 5722h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4990 5722h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.53,-0.19&amp;year=1939">What was here in 1939? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
